::CW_U03_UA_Q01::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q01
Passage: A character gives up a winning speech slot to help a nervous friend prepare. What universal theme is best supported?{
~A. Speeches are always short.#Incorrect. Length is not the focus.
=B. Personal goals can still leave room for helping others.#Correct. The character sacrifices attention to support a friend.
~C. Friendship prevents all ambition.#Incorrect. The theme is balance, not removing ambition.
~D. Winning never matters.#Incorrect. The slot matters because giving it up is a sacrifice.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q02::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q02
Source element: A guide gives an object before a journey. Adaptation: A coach gives a route card before a city race. What is preserved?{
~A. The exact original setting.#Incorrect. The setting changes.
~B. A copied plot.#Incorrect. The scene is transformed.
~C. No source connection.#Incorrect. The source pattern remains.
=D. A guide supports a journey with an object.#Correct. The coach and route card preserve the guide-object pattern.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q03::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.04
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q03
Central idea: Avoiding responsibility can make guilt grow. Which detail best supports it?{
=A. The character hides the broken key after blaming someone else.#Correct. Hiding evidence after blame shows guilt.
~B. The wall is painted white.#Incorrect. This is unrelated by itself.
~C. A dog walks outside.#Incorrect. This does not connect to guilt.
~D. The character eats breakfast.#Incorrect. This does not support responsibility.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q04::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q04
Passage: "Naturally, the committee solved the leak by buying a larger bucket." What rhetorical choice is strongest?{
~A. Neutral admiration.#Incorrect. The tone is critical.
~B. A scientific report.#Incorrect. The passage is rhetorical.
=C. Sarcasm criticizing an ineffective solution.#Correct. The sentence mocks the committee's poor response.
~D. A theme comparison.#Incorrect. The question asks rhetoric.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q05::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q05
Passage: Eli thinks family stories delay chores until he finds the same street names in his grandmother's old notebook. What changes?{
=A. Eli recognizes that the stories carry family history.#Correct. The notebook changes his perspective.
~B. Eli proves chores are impossible.#Incorrect. Chores are not the main realization.
~C. Eli stops listening forever.#Incorrect. The evidence suggests he values the stories.
~D. The notebook has no role.#Incorrect. It provides evidence.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q06::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q06
Passage A: Lina practices alone after failure. Passage B: Theo asks for help after failure. What comparison is strongest?{
~A. The passages are identical because both have names.#Incorrect. Names are surface details.
~B. Only Passage A has a theme.#Incorrect. Both have themes.
~C. Failure ends growth.#Incorrect. Both continue.
=D. Both show growth after failure, but one emphasizes independent persistence while the other emphasizes support.#Correct. This compares shared theme and different development.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q07::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q07
Passage: Talia thinks asking for critique proves weakness, but later asks what to revise first. What coming-of-age change is shown?{
~A. She refuses all revision.#Incorrect. She asks what to revise.
=B. She learns feedback can be part of growth.#Correct. Her belief changes.
~C. She stops making art.#Incorrect. No evidence says that.
~D. She avoids the teacher.#Incorrect. She asks the teacher.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q08::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q08
What is a complete theme statement?{
~A. Friendship.#Incorrect. This is only a topic.
~B. The story has a friend.#Incorrect. This is summary.
=C. Friendship can require honesty even when honesty is uncomfortable.#Correct. This is a complete idea.
~D. Blue jacket.#Incorrect. This is a detail.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q09::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q09
A student adapts a source by changing only character names while keeping the exact plot and ending. What is the problem?{
~A. It changes too much.#Incorrect. It changes very little.
~B. It has no source connection.#Incorrect. It is overly close to the source.
~C. It is automatically respectful.#Incorrect. Respect also requires careful representation and purpose.
=D. It is too close to copying and lacks meaningful transformation.#Correct. Adaptation requires purposeful change.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q10::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.04
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q10
Passage: The clock ticks louder each time Ren avoids the question. What is the reader effect?{
=A. The sound detail increases tension around avoidance.#Correct. The ticking emphasizes delay.
~B. The clock explains math.#Incorrect. No math is involved.
~C. The question disappears.#Incorrect. The question remains central.
~D. The scene becomes cheerful.#Incorrect. The mood is tense.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q11::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q11
Central idea: Memory can make a new place feel less lonely. Which revision best supports it?{
~A. Add unrelated traffic.#Incorrect. This distracts.
~B. Remove every memory detail.#Incorrect. That weakens the idea.
=C. Change "blue rug" to "a blue rug the same color as Grandma's kitchen rug."#Correct. The revised detail connects memory to place.
~D. List random furniture.#Incorrect. Random detail does not support the idea.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q12::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q12
Passage: A narrator repeats "not yet" while postponing the truth. What author purpose is likely?{
~A. To remove reader effect.#Incorrect. Repetition creates effect.
=B. To emphasize avoidance through repetition.#Correct. The repeated phrase mirrors delay.
~C. To explain cooking.#Incorrect. No cooking appears.
~D. To prove the truth was told early.#Incorrect. It is postponed.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q13::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q13
Which detail best supports "courage can exist beside fear"?{
=A. Kai steps forward while hiding shaking hands.#Correct. The action shows fear and courage together.
~B. Kai feels no fear at all.#Incorrect. That removes the contrast.
~C. Kai never moves.#Incorrect. Courage requires action here.
~D. Kai changes socks.#Incorrect. Unrelated.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q14::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q14
Source element: A forbidden door tests curiosity. Adaptation: A student is warned not to open a locked digital folder. What is preserved?{
~A. The exact physical door.#Incorrect. It becomes a digital folder.
~B. No conflict.#Incorrect. The warning creates conflict.
~C. The same time period.#Incorrect. It is modernized.
=D. The forbidden-choice test.#Correct. The door changes form, but the test remains.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q15::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q15
Passage: A character returns a trophy after learning the score was wrong. What theme is strongest?{
~A. Trophies are always made of gold.#Incorrect. Material is not mentioned.
~B. Scores never matter.#Incorrect. The score matters.
=C. Doing what is right can matter more than public praise.#Correct. The character gives up an undeserved reward.
~D. Honesty removes all conflict.#Incorrect. Honesty creates a difficult choice.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q16::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q16
Which explanation best connects evidence to central idea? Central idea: Secrets damage trust. Detail: The character deletes a message before a friend sees it.{
~A. It supports the idea because phones exist.#Incorrect. The phone alone is not enough.
=B. The deleted message supports the idea because hiding communication creates secrecy between friends.#Correct. This explains the connection.
~C. It supports the idea because it is a sentence.#Incorrect. That explains nothing.
~D. Details do not need connection.#Incorrect. Support requires connection.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q17::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.04
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q17
Passage: "Of course the award went to the student who arrived late and bowed like royalty." What tone is created?{
~A. Completely neutral.#Incorrect. The sentence has attitude.
~B. Scientific.#Incorrect. It is not scientific.
~C. Grateful.#Incorrect. It does not sound grateful.
=D. Sarcastic and critical.#Correct. The wording criticizes the award choice.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q18::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q18
What should a rationale for adaptation include?{
=A. Source element, transformation, and reason for the change.#Correct. These explain the adaptation.
~B. Only a new name.#Incorrect. A name is not enough.
~C. No explanation.#Incorrect. A rationale explains.
~D. Only punctuation.#Incorrect. Adaptation is about craft choices.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q19::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q19
Passage: A character thinks leadership means doing everything alone, then succeeds only after trusting the team. What central idea is best supported?{
~A. Teams always fail.#Incorrect. The team helps.
=B. Leadership can require shared responsibility.#Correct. The character changes through teamwork.
~C. Leaders should never trust anyone.#Incorrect. The passage shows the opposite.
~D. Responsibility is unrelated.#Incorrect. It is central.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q20::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q20
Passage: A narrator leaves home but packs a recipe card first. What theme is best supported?{
~A. Recipes erase memory.#Incorrect. The card preserves memory.
~B. Moving means forgetting everything.#Incorrect. The narrator carries memory.
=C. Change can include carrying meaningful parts of the past forward.#Correct. The card preserves memory during change.
~D. Home is never important.#Incorrect. The card shows home matters.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q21::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q21
What is a blind spot in coming-of-age fiction?{
=A. Something a character does not yet understand.#Correct. Growth often begins when the blind spot is challenged.
~B. A required eye injury.#Incorrect. It is about understanding.
~C. A random setting detail.#Incorrect. It is a perspective limit.
~D. A complete theme statement.#Incorrect. It is not a theme statement.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q22::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q22
Passage: The writer gives the antagonist a quiet moment helping a child. What purpose might this serve?{
~A. To remove all conflict.#Incorrect. Complexity can deepen conflict.
~B. To prove the character has no role.#Incorrect. It deepens the role.
=C. To complicate the reader's view of the character.#Correct. A humane action adds complexity.
~D. To change the passage into a recipe.#Incorrect. No recipe appears.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q23::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q23
Which comparison is strongest? Passage A: a child returns a stolen coin. Passage B: a merchant corrects an overpayment.{
~A. Coins and merchants are identical.#Incorrect. They are different details.
~B. Only children can be honest.#Incorrect. The merchant is honest too.
~C. Money removes theme.#Incorrect. Money creates the integrity conflict.
=D. Both develop integrity through returning what does not belong to the character.#Correct. Both actions restore fairness.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q24::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.04
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q24
Scene plan: central idea about belonging, conflict about exclusion, and detail of a shared table. What makes it integrated?{
~A. It uses unrelated details.#Incorrect. Integration requires connection.
=B. The conflict and table detail both support the belonging idea.#Correct. The pieces point to the same meaning.
~C. The table has no role.#Incorrect. It can symbolize inclusion.
~D. The central idea should be removed.#Incorrect. It guides the scene.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q25::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q25
A student writes, "The theme is that Kai finds a coin." What feedback is best?{
=A. Revise because that is a plot detail; explain the larger idea the coin supports.#Correct. Theme must be a complete idea.
~B. Keep it because all plot details are themes.#Incorrect. Details support themes.
~C. Remove all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is needed.
~D. Use one word only.#Incorrect. One-word topics are not complete themes.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q26::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q26
Passage: Lena wanted to ignore the new student because everyone else did. At lunch, she moved her tray to the empty seat beside him. Which theme statement is strongest?{
~A. Lunch can happen at school.#Incorrect. This is a fact from the situation, not a theme.
~B. The topic is students.#Incorrect. "Students" is a topic, not a complete theme statement.
=C. Belonging often begins when one person chooses kindness over comfort.#Correct. This is a complete universal idea supported by Lena's choice.
~D. Lena has a tray.#Incorrect. This only names a detail and does not explain the larger meaning.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q27::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q27
Passage: Tomas wanted to win the art prize, but he helped his tired partner finish her mural before starting his own final details. What topic and theme are best supported?{
=A. Topic: ambition and teamwork; Theme: Personal goals can still leave room for helping others.#Correct. Tomas wants to win, yet he chooses to help his partner.
~B. Topic: paint; Theme: Paint is colorful.#Incorrect. The passage is not mainly about paint color.
~C. Topic: sleep; Theme: Everyone should nap.#Incorrect. Tiredness is a detail, not the central theme.
~D. Topic: prizes; Theme: Prizes always ruin friendships.#Incorrect. The passage does not show prizes always causing harm.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q28::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q28
Passage: Asha kept the cracked bracelet after her friend moved away. She wore it on the first day at a new school, touching it before she introduced herself. What does the bracelet help develop?{
~A. A theme about jewelry prices.#Incorrect. The passage never focuses on cost.
~B. A rule that all bracelets are cracked.#Incorrect. That is not a life idea or theme.
~C. A summary that Asha has a bracelet.#Incorrect. A summary is not a theme explanation.
=D. A theme about memory giving strength during change.#Correct. The bracelet connects friendship, memory, and Asha's courage in a new place.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q29::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q29
Passage A: Rina gave her last pencil to a classmate before the exam. Passage B: Omar shared his umbrella even though his own sleeve got soaked. What shared theme is best supported?{
~A. School supplies and umbrellas are the same object.#Incorrect. The objects differ; the theme connection is generosity.
~B. Weather is always more important than school.#Incorrect. Only Passage B includes weather.
=C. Helping others can require giving up personal comfort or convenience.#Correct. Both characters sacrifice something to help another person.
~D. Exams should happen in the rain.#Incorrect. This combines details in a way the passages do not support.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q30::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q30
Passage A: Mina apologized after blaming her brother for the spill. Passage B: Carter returned the borrowed book after pretending he had lost it. How are the themes similar?{
=A. Both show that honesty can repair damage after a mistake.#Correct. Both characters admit or correct a dishonest action.
~B. Both are mainly about cleaning.#Incorrect. Only Passage A includes a spill, and cleaning is not the theme.
~C. Both show that books are dangerous.#Incorrect. The book is part of one conflict, not the shared theme.
~D. Both prove apologies are useless.#Incorrect. The passages show movement toward repair.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q31::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.04
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q31
Source element: A character receives a warning before making a risky choice. Adaptation: A student ignores a teammate's warning about faulty robot code before the final round. What is preserved?{
~A. The exact original setting.#Incorrect. The setting has been transformed.
~B. The character names from the source.#Incorrect. No original names are being preserved.
=C. The warning before a risky choice.#Correct. The adaptation keeps the warning-and-choice pattern.
~D. The complete original plot.#Incorrect. The adaptation changes the plot situation.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q32::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.04
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q32
Source element: A guide gives a traveler a protective object. Adaptation: A grandmother gives a narrator a recipe card before the narrator moves to a new city. What is transformed?{
=A. The protective object becomes emotional support through a recipe card.#Correct. The source object changes into a realistic family object with emotional meaning.
~B. The source is copied exactly.#Incorrect. The object and situation are changed.
~C. The adaptation removes all source connection.#Incorrect. A guide gives an object before a difficult journey.
~D. The character has no journey.#Incorrect. Moving to a new city is a kind of journey.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q33::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q33
Central idea: Avoiding responsibility can make guilt grow. Passage detail: Niko hides the baseball under three blankets after blaming the broken window on the wind. How does the detail support the central idea?{
~A. It shows Niko likes blankets.#Incorrect. The blankets matter because he uses them to hide evidence.
~B. It proves the wind broke the window.#Incorrect. The hidden baseball suggests responsibility.
=C. It shows Niko's guilt growing through secretive action.#Correct. The hiding shows avoidance becoming more intense.
~D. It distracts from the central idea completely.#Incorrect. The detail directly supports the idea.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q34::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q34
Central idea: Accepting help can be a form of strength. Which detail best supports the idea?{
=A. The character hands one heavy box to a friend and keeps walking beside her.#Correct. The action shows accepting help without giving up.
~B. The character describes an unrelated poster for three paragraphs.#Incorrect. This does not connect to accepting help.
~C. The character refuses all help and leaves.#Incorrect. This works against the central idea.
~D. The character counts clouds.#Incorrect. This is unrelated unless tied to the conflict.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q35::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q35
Passage: The writer repeats the image of an empty chair at dinner before explaining who is missing. What is the most likely purpose?{
~A. To describe furniture only.#Incorrect. The repeated chair creates meaning beyond furniture.
~B. To prove the chair is new.#Incorrect. Age or condition is not the focus.
=C. To make the reader feel absence before receiving full explanation.#Correct. Repetition builds mood and curiosity.
~D. To avoid all emotion.#Incorrect. The image creates emotion.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q36::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q36
Passage: "Of course the award went to the student who forgot the speech and bowed like royalty." What craft choice is strongest?{
=A. Sarcastic tone.#Correct. "Of course" and "bowed like royalty" create criticism.
~B. Warm admiration.#Incorrect. The wording sounds critical, not admiring.
~C. Neutral listing.#Incorrect. The sentence has attitude.
~D. Scientific explanation.#Incorrect. This is rhetorical tone, not science.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q37::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q37
Passage: Talia thinks asking for critique proves she is not ready for advanced art. After her teacher explains that artists revise through feedback, Talia asks what to change first. What is Talia's changed understanding?{
~A. Artists never revise.#Incorrect. The teacher says artists revise.
~B. Asking questions proves failure.#Incorrect. The passage shows the opposite.
=C. Feedback can be part of serious growth.#Correct. Talia learns that asking for help supports improvement.
~D. Advanced art has no challenge.#Incorrect. The class is challenging.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q38::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q38
Passage: Marco thinks his father's traffic rules are about control. After his sister almost rides into the street, he understands why his father checks twice. What causes Marco's change?{
=A. He sees the safety reason behind the rule.#Correct. The near-accident changes his perspective.
~B. He forgets the rule.#Incorrect. He understands it better.
~C. His father disappears.#Incorrect. The father remains part of the conflict.
~D. The street becomes irrelevant.#Incorrect. The street creates the danger.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q39::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q39
Scene plan: Central idea: maturity includes accepting guidance. Source influence: a guide gives an object before a journey. Character conflict: Maya thinks leaders work alone, while her teammate thinks leaders trust the group. Which choice best explains integration?{
~A. The plan is integrated because it has many unrelated parts.#Incorrect. Integration requires connection, not just many parts.
~B. The source influence distracts because objects can never develop theme.#Incorrect. Objects can support theme when connected to conflict and growth.
=C. The plan is integrated because every element supports shared responsibility.#Correct. The central idea, guide-object pattern, and perspective conflict all point to the same meaning.
~D. The conflict should be removed so the theme is easier.#Incorrect. Conflict helps develop the theme.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q40::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q40
Passage: Jalen believed the mural had to be his design alone. When Amara handed him a sketch showing how everyone's ideas could fit, he erased his signature from the center and redrew the wall as a shared map. What changed understanding is shown?{
=A. Jalen learns that leadership can include shared creative ownership.#Correct. He moves from control to collaboration.
~B. Jalen learns maps are never useful.#Incorrect. The shared map becomes meaningful.
~C. Jalen decides art has no theme.#Incorrect. The mural develops theme.
~D. Jalen avoids all collaboration.#Incorrect. He accepts Amara's input.
}
